{
  // Typechecking pass for the test suites only.
  //
  // Tests are run by `node --test`, which executes .ts files directly via
  // Node 24's native type stripping — that strips types, it does not check
  // them, so without this config a test could reference a field that no
  // longer exists and still "pass". This config is the checking half.
  //
  // ONE IMPORT RULE, everywhere: a test imports workspace packages by name
  // ("@vouch/db"), never by a relative path into any package's src/.
  //
  // Week 1 did the opposite, to keep `npm test` build-free. That only worked
  // by accident: every intra-package import in the files under test was
  // `import type` and was erased before Node saw it. Week 2's packages have
  // real value imports between their own files (store.ts -> schema.js), and
  // Node's type stripping does NOT rewrite a ".js" specifier to a ".ts"
  // file, so those modules cannot be loaded from src/ at all.
  //
  // Importing the built package also fixes a second problem it would have
  // been easy to paper over: a class with a private field is nominally
  // typed, so a VouchStore constructed from ../../db/src is not assignable
  // to the "@vouch/db" VouchStore in VouchService's signature.
  //
  // Consequences: `npm test` runs `tsc -b` first, and passes
  // --enable-source-maps so failures still report TypeScript line numbers.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    // composite and noEmit are mutually exclusive; the base config turns
    // composite on for the package build graph, which this pass is not part of.
    "composite": false,
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  // Only the test files are listed; the source they import is pulled in
  // transitively and checked along with them.
  // Operational scripts are included here too. They live outside any
  // package's build `include` (they are tools, not library code, and nothing
  // should be able to import them), so without this line they would be the
  // only TypeScript in the repo that is never typechecked — and they are
  // exactly the code you run once, under time pressure, against a live
  // third-party service.
  "include": ["test/**/*.ts", "packages/*/test/**/*.ts", "packages/*/scripts/**/*.ts"]
}
